
var TAT={marx:{n:'Karl Marx',i:'M',y:'1818–1883',l:'Follow the money. Who owns, who works, who profits?'},
 durk:{n:'Émile Durkheim',i:'D',y:'1858–1917',l:'What holds people together? Shared rituals, rules and feelings.'},
 weber:{n:'Max Weber',i:'W',y:'1864–1920',l:'What does it mean to the people doing it? Status, authority, calculation.'},
 sri:{n:'M. N. Srinivas',i:'S',y:'1916–1999',l:'Look from the Indian village up: caste, imitation, mobility.'}};
var TAK=['marx','durk','weber','sri'];
/* event: id, t title, sc scene, then per thinker [concept, argument, what it misses] */
var TA=[
{id:'ipl',t:'The IPL auction',sc:'A hammer falls and a 20-year-old from a small town is sold for ₹10 crore while millions watch live. Franchises owned by business houses bid for players like prized assets.',
 marx:['Commodification','Look at the words: bought, sold, owned. The player’s skill has become a commodity, and owners and broadcasters keep most of the value his labour creates. The cheering fan pays for it all through tickets and adverts.','A player on ₹10 crore is hardly an exploited worker, and fans love the game for its own sake.'],
 durk:['Collective effervescence','A team jersey is a modern totem. When a stadium chants together, people feel part of something bigger than themselves, and that shared excitement renews the bonds of a city.','Says little about who makes the money or who is left out.'],
 weber:['Rationalisation','Notice the spreadsheets: strike rates, salary caps, data analysts. Sport has been rationalised into a calculated business. And the price tag gives a player status, which is separate from his wealth or power.','Explains the system’s logic but not the raw passion of the crowd.'],
 sri:['Social mobility','A boy from a small town and a modest background becomes a national hero. Cricket, a British game, is now a ladder of mobility that village India understands. Watch whose families rise in local standing.','Mobility for a few stars does not change the structure for everyone else.']},
{id:'wrapped',t:'Spotify Wrapped day',sc:'Every December, millions post colourful cards showing their top songs and artists. Friends compare, tease and judge each other’s taste.',
 marx:['Data as commodity','Your listening is unpaid work. Every play produces data that the company sells to advertisers, and Wrapped turns you into a free billboard. Meanwhile the artist earns a fraction of a rupee per stream.','People are not fooled. They enjoy sharing and discovering music.'],
 durk:['Ritual and solidarity','It is an annual ritual. On the same day everyone performs the same act, and the shared symbols, a favourite band or genre, sort people into small tribes that feel like belonging.','Ignores that the ritual was designed by a company to sell subscriptions.'],
 weber:['Status group','Taste marks a status group: what you listen to signals the kind of person you claim to be. And see how even something as personal as music has been counted, ranked and turned into statistics.','Not every listener is performing. Some just like the song.'],
 sri:['Westernisation','Watch who imitates whom. Small-town listeners pick up English pop and K-pop, much as groups once adopted the customs of those above them, yet regional film songs still top most Indian lists. Westernisation is real but selective.','A framework built for caste and village may be stretched too far here.']},
{id:'coaching',t:'The coaching town',sc:'Teenagers leave home for a town full of coaching institutes, study twelve hours a day and see their rank posted on a board every week.',
 marx:['Class reproduction','Education has become an industry, and fees decide who may enter the race. Families with capital buy an advantage, and everyone else is told the contest is fair. Class is reproduced and called merit.','Many first-generation students really do rise through these tests.'],
 durk:['Anomie','Children are pulled away from family and friends and tied only to a rank. When old bonds weaken and goals have no limit, people feel anomie, a painful loss of norms. Society, and not only the individual, must answer for that stress.','Underplays the economics of who can afford to be there at all.'],
 weber:['Iron cage','Modern society selects people by examination and certificate. That is legal-rational authority at work. But a life organised wholly around scores becomes an iron cage of calculation.','Describes the cage well but says little about how to change it.'],
 sri:['New routes to status','For many families a professional degree is the new route to the standing that land or ritual rank once gave. Notice which communities send children, how hostels cluster by region, and how one success raises a whole family’s status at home.','A focus on community can miss the student’s own ambition.']},
{id:'influencer',t:'A million followers',sc:'A college student posts dance reels. Within a year there are a million followers, brand deals and strangers copying the style.',
 marx:['Alienated labour','The followers’ attention is the product being sold. The platform owns the means of distribution and takes its cut, while the creator works without wages or security. One change to the algorithm can end the income.','The creator also owns a small brand, so this is not simply a worker.'],
 durk:['Social sanctions','Likes are tiny acts of approval: society’s sanctions made visible. Trends show how strongly the group shapes what each person wears, says and even feels.','Cannot easily explain people who deliberately break the trend.'],
 weber:['Charismatic authority','This is charismatic authority. People follow because of personal appeal, not because of a post or a tradition. Charisma is unstable, so it must be turned into routine: schedules, contracts, a team.','The charisma is partly manufactured by the algorithm.'],
 sri:['Reference models','Here is a new reference model. Young people in small towns copy the dress and speech, much as groups once imitated the dominant caste of their village. The direction of imitation shows where prestige now lies.','Online prestige may not change real standing at home.']},
{id:'wedding',t:'The big wedding',sc:'A family spends years of savings on a five-day wedding with a destination venue, designer clothes and a thousand guests.',
 marx:['Market and property','A wedding has become a market. Planners, jewellers and lenders profit, and families go into debt to display wealth they may not have. Marriage also keeps property inside a class.','People value the ceremony for love and family, not only for display.'],
 durk:['Ritual','Rituals bind. A wedding gathers scattered relatives, renews kinship ties and publicly marks a change of status. The expense shows how much the group matters.','Does not ask who bears the cost, which is often the bride’s family.'],
 weber:['Status honour','Much of it is traditional action, done because it has always been done, mixed with a search for status honour. A lavish style is how a status group shows where it stands.','Cannot fully separate real belief from showing off.'],
 sri:['Sanskritisation','Look at which customs are being adopted. Families rising in wealth take up the rituals, and sadly sometimes the dowry practices, of groups above them. That is Sanskritisation, now mixed with film-style Westernisation.','Today’s models come as much from films and social media as from higher castes.']},
{id:'gig',t:'The delivery rider',sc:'A young man leaves his village, rents a bike in the city and delivers food for an app that sets his pay and rates him by stars.',
 marx:['Alienation','He owns nothing but his labour, and the app owns the market. He never meets his employer and cannot bargain, and the surplus of his work goes to shareholders. This is alienation with a smartphone.','Some riders value the flexibility and earn more than they could in the village.'],
 durk:['Organic solidarity','The city runs on organic solidarity: strangers who depend on each other’s specialised work. But if rules and protections do not keep up with this new division of labour, it turns anomic.','Assumes that depending on each other creates solidarity. Riders may simply feel invisible.'],
 weber:['Bureaucracy','The algorithm is bureaucracy perfected: impersonal rules, constant measurement and no face to appeal to. His life chances depend on his position in the market.','Says little about the protests riders have begun to organise together.'],
 sri:['Village–city link','He has not left the village behind. He sends money home, returns for festivals and probably found the job through someone from his own caste or district. City and village are one system.','City life can also loosen caste ties in ways this view underrates.']},
{id:'final',t:'The night India wins',sc:'India wins a World Cup final. Within minutes the streets fill with strangers hugging, dancing and waving flags until dawn.',
 marx:['Spectacle','For one night the factory owner and the worker cheer together, and the differences between them are forgotten. Spectacle can work like an opiate while the sponsors count their profits.','Joy is not false just because someone profits from it.'],
 durk:['Collective effervescence','This is collective effervescence. In the crowd, individuals feel a force larger than themselves. The flag is the sacred symbol, and through it the nation celebrates its own unity.','It is unity for a night. The divisions return in the morning.'],
 weber:['Affectual action','This is affectual action, driven by emotion and not by calculation. The nation is a community of shared feeling and prestige, and a victory raises the honour of everyone who belongs.','Describes the emotion, but why cricket and not hockey needs history.'],
 sri:['Unity and hierarchy','In the village everyone watches on one screen, and old lines of caste and faction blur for an evening. But note who sits where, and whose house has the television. Unity and hierarchy exist together.','A village lens may miss how urban and anonymous these crowds are.']},
{id:'uniform',t:'The school uniform',sc:'Every morning a thousand students put on the same clothes, stand in rows and move when the bell rings.',
 marx:['Training for work','School trains future workers: punctuality, obedience, a bell for every task. The uniform hides class for a few hours, but the shoes, the tuition and the school itself reveal it.','Schools also give poor children real tools to rise.'],
 durk:['Socialisation','School is where society makes its members. The uniform and the assembly teach children that they belong to something beyond the family and must follow common rules.','Too trusting that the rules being taught are fair to everyone.'],
 weber:['Legal-rational authority','Timetables, registers, ranks and rules: the school is a small bureaucracy. The principal is obeyed because of the office, not the person.','Leaves out the friendships and informal groups that make school life.'],
 sri:['Caste and change','Sitting on the same bench and eating together broke old rules of purity and distance. Schooling was one of the great engines of change in caste India. Still, look at who attends which school.','Shared benches did not end inequality outside the gate.']}
];
var TAV={ans:[],busy:false};
function taSt(){S.ta=S.ta||{};S.ta.p=S.ta.p||{};S.ta.cu=S.ta.cu||[];return S.ta}
function taAll(){return TA.concat(taSt().cu)}
function taEv(id){return taAll().filter(function(e){return e.id===id})[0]}
function taOpen(id){SJV.ta={ev:id||null};TAV={ans:[],busy:false};view='sj_soc';SJV.p=null;SJV.rv=null;SJV.qz=null;SJV.mm=null;render();window.scrollTo(0,0)}
function taLean(){var p=taSt().p,n={},t=0;TAK.forEach(function(k){n[k]=0});Object.keys(p).forEach(function(e){if(n[p[e]]!=null&&taEv(e)){n[p[e]]++;t++}});return{n:n,t:t}}
function taView(){var ev=SJV.ta.ev&&taEv(SJV.ta.ev);if(ev)return taEvent(ev);SJV.ta.ev=null;
  var st=taSt(),L=taLean(),hasC=typeof claude!=='undefined'&&claude&&claude.use;
  var h='<p class="back"><button class="link" onclick="SJV.ta=null;render();window.scrollTo(0,0)">← Sociology</button></p><h1>Thinkers argue</h1><p class="sub">Four sociologists look at the same everyday event and disagree. Read their arguments, decide who convinces you, then see what each view misses.</p>';
  h+='<div class="tlens">'+TAK.map(function(k){var T=TAT[k];return'<div class="tk '+k+'"><b>'+T.n+'</b><small>'+T.y+'</small><p>'+esc(T.l)+'</p></div>'}).join('')+'</div>';
  if(L.t){var top=TAK.slice().sort(function(a,b){return L.n[b]-L.n[a]})[0];
    h+='<div class="card"><b>Your leaning so far</b><div class="tlean">'+TAK.map(function(k){return L.n[k]?'<i class="tk '+k+'" style="width:'+(L.n[k]/L.t*100)+'%"></i>':''}).join('')+'</div><div class="tleank">'+TAK.map(function(k){return'<span class="tk '+k+'"><i></i>'+TAT[k].n.split(' ').pop()+' '+L.n[k]+'</span>'}).join('')+'</div>'+(L.t>=3?'<p class="sub" style="margin:8px 0 0">You side most often with '+TAT[top].n+'. Try reading the next event through a different thinker’s eyes.</p>':'')+'</div>'}
  h+='<div class="card"><b>Pick an event</b>'+taAll().map(function(e){var p=st.p[e.id];return'<button class="tev" onclick="taOpen(\''+e.id+'\')"><span class="tt"><b>'+esc(e.t)+'</b><small class="muted">'+esc(e.sc.split('. ')[0])+'.</small></span>'+(p?'<span class="pill ok">You chose '+TAT[p].n.split(' ').pop()+'</span>':'<span class="pill">New</span>')+'</button>'}).join('')+'</div>';
  h+='<div class="card"><b>Bring your own event</b><p class="sub" style="margin:4px 0 8px">Something from the news, school, a film or a song. Claude writes how each thinker would argue about it.</p>'
    +(hasC?'<form class="ask" style="margin-top:0" onsubmit="event.preventDefault();taMake()"><input id="taNew" placeholder="e.g. A K-pop concert sells out in ten minutes" autocomplete="off" aria-label="Your event" maxlength="160"><button class="chipb" type="submit">Go</button></form><div id="taNm" class="sub" style="margin:6px 0 0"></div>':'<p class="sub" style="margin:0">Open the app inside Claude to use this.</p>')+'</div>';
  h+='<p class="tnote">These are imagined arguments built from each thinker’s real ideas. They are not quotations.</p>';
  app.innerHTML=h}
function taEvent(e){var st=taSt(),p=st.p[e.id],hasC=typeof claude!=='undefined'&&claude&&claude.use;
  var h='<p class="back"><button class="link" onclick="SJV.ta.ev=null;render();window.scrollTo(0,0)">← All events</button></p><div class="eyebrow">Thinkers argue</div><h1>'+esc(e.t)+'</h1><div class="card"><p class="tscene">'+esc(e.sc)+'</p></div>';
  h+=TAK.map(function(k){var T=TAT[k],a=e[k];return'<div class="take tk '+k+(p===k?' won':'')+'"><span class="tav" aria-hidden="true">'+T.i+'</span><div class="tb"><div class="th"><b>'+T.n+'</b><span class="ttag">'+esc(a[0])+'</span></div><p>'+esc(a[1])+'</p>'+(p?'<div class="tmiss"><b>What this misses:</b> '+esc(a[2])+'</div>':'')+'</div></div>'}).join('');
  h+='<div class="card"><b>'+(p?'You sided with '+TAT[p].n+'.':'Who convinces you most?')+'</b><p class="sub" style="margin:4px 0 8px">'+(p?'Each argument above now shows its weak point. You can change your mind.':'Choose one. Then each argument shows its weak point.')+'</p><div class="tpick">'+TAK.map(function(k){return'<button class="tk '+k+(p===k?' on':'')+'" onclick="taPick(\''+e.id+'\',\''+k+'\')">'+TAT[k].n.split(' ').pop()+'</button>'}).join('')+'</div></div>';
  if(p)h+='<div class="card"><b>Keep the argument going</b>'+(hasC?'<div class="chips">'+['Challenge my choice','Let two of them argue it out','Which chapter ideas are at work here?'].map(function(q,i){return'<button class="chipb" onclick="taAsk('+i+')">'+q+'</button>'}).join('')+'</div><form class="ask" onsubmit="event.preventDefault();taAsk(-1)"><input id="taQ" placeholder="Argue back, or ask a thinker something" autocomplete="off" aria-label="Your reply"><button class="chipb" type="submit">Send</button></form><div id="taA">'+taAns()+'</div>':'<p class="sub" style="margin:6px 0 0">Open the app inside Claude to debate.</p>')+'</div>';
  var A=taAll(),i=A.indexOf(e),nx=A[(i+1)%A.length];
  h+='<button class="btn alt" style="text-align:center" onclick="taOpen(\''+nx.id+'\')">Next event: '+esc(nx.t)+' →</button>'+(e.cu?'<button class="btn alt ntdel" style="text-align:center" onclick="taDel(\''+e.id+'\')">Remove this event</button>':'');
  h+='<p class="tnote" style="margin-top:14px">Imagined arguments built from each thinker’s real ideas, not quotations.</p>';
  app.innerHTML=h}
function taPick(id,k){taSt().p[id]=k;save();var y=window.scrollY;render();window.scrollTo(0,y)}
function taDel(id){var st=taSt();st.cu=st.cu.filter(function(e){return e.id!==id});delete st.p[id];save();taOpen()}
function taAns(){return TAV.ans.map(function(x){return'<div class="cmsg"><b>'+esc(x.q)+'</b><div class="ctext">'+(x.a==null?'<span class="muted">Claude is writing…</span>':esc(x.a))+'</div></div>'}).join('')}
var TAQ=['Challenge my choice: let the thinker who disagrees most with my pick argue against it, in their own voice.','Write a short back-and-forth between the two thinkers who disagree most about this event, three turns each.','Name the ideas from my sociology chapters that are at work in this event, and say which thinker each belongs to.'];
var TAQL=['Challenge my choice','Let two of them argue it out','Which chapter ideas are at work here?'];
async function taAsk(i){var e=SJV.ta&&taEv(SJV.ta.ev);if(!e||TAV.busy)return;var p=taSt().p[e.id],inp=document.getElementById('taQ'),own=i<0?(inp&&inp.value||'').trim():'';if(i<0&&!own)return;
  var it={q:i<0?own:TAQL[i],a:null};TAV.ans.push(it);TAV.busy=true;if(inp)inp.value='';var o=document.getElementById('taA');if(o){o.innerHTML=taAns();o.lastChild&&o.lastChild.scrollIntoView({block:'nearest'})}
  try{var s=await getS();if(!s)throw new Error('none');
    var d=await s.json('You make sociology enjoyable for a senior-school student through an imagined debate between Karl Marx, Émile Durkheim, Max Weber and M. N. Srinivas about an everyday event. Stay faithful to each thinker’s real ideas and name the concept being used. You may write in a thinker’s voice, but label whose voice it is and never present the words as real quotations. Be lively, fair to every side, and end with one short question back to the student. Use neutral wording for the student and never assume their gender, class or school. Plain text only, no markdown, at most 170 words.\nEVENT: '+e.t+'. '+e.sc+'\n'+TAK.map(function(k){return TAT[k].n.toUpperCase()+' ('+e[k][0]+'): '+e[k][1]}).join('\n')+'\nTHE STUDENT SIDED WITH: '+(p?TAT[p].n:'nobody yet')+'\nEARLIER IN THIS DEBATE: '+TAV.ans.slice(0,-1).map(function(a){return'Student: '+a.q+' / Reply: '+(a.a||'').slice(0,300)}).join(' | ')+'\nTHE STUDENT NOW SAYS OR ASKS: '+(i<0?own:TAQ[i])+'\nReturn JSON only: {"answer": "your reply"}',{cache:false,modelTier:'default'});
    it.a=String(d&&d.answer||'Claude had nothing to add just now. Try another way.')}
  catch(err){it.a=err&&err.code?sampleErr(err):'Claude couldn’t reply just now. Try again in a moment.'}
  TAV.busy=false;var o2=document.getElementById('taA');if(o2){o2.innerHTML=taAns();o2.lastChild&&o2.lastChild.scrollIntoView({block:'nearest'})}}
async function taMake(){var inp=document.getElementById('taNew'),m=document.getElementById('taNm'),t=(inp&&inp.value||'').trim();if(!t||TAV.busy)return;TAV.busy=true;if(m)m.textContent='Claude is writing the four arguments…';
  try{var s=await getS();if(!s)throw new Error('none');
    var d=await s.json('A senior-school sociology student wants to see how four thinkers would argue about an everyday event. Write imagined arguments that stay faithful to each thinker’s real ideas: Karl Marx, Émile Durkheim, Max Weber and M. N. Srinivas. Each argument is 2 or 3 short sentences in that thinker’s voice, names one of their concepts, and comes with one sentence on what that view misses. If the event is unsuitable for a school student, or is about a named private person, set "ok" to false. Plain text, no markdown.\nEVENT: '+t+'\nReturn JSON only: {"ok": true, "title": "short title, at most 6 words", "scene": "the event in 2 vivid sentences", "marx": {"concept": "", "says": "", "misses": ""}, "durkheim": {"concept": "", "says": "", "misses": ""}, "weber": {"concept": "", "says": "", "misses": ""}, "srinivas": {"concept": "", "says": "", "misses": ""}}',{cache:false,modelTier:'default'});
    if(!d||d.ok===false)throw new Error('no');var g=function(x){if(!x||!x.says)throw new Error('shape');return[String(x.concept||'Idea'),String(x.says),String(x.misses||'')]};
    var e={id:'cu'+Date.now(),cu:1,t:String(d.title||t).slice(0,60),sc:String(d.scene||t),marx:g(d.marx),durk:g(d.durkheim),weber:g(d.weber),sri:g(d.srinivas)},st=taSt();
    st.cu.push(e);if(st.cu.length>6){var old=st.cu.shift();delete st.p[old.id]}save();TAV.busy=false;taOpen(e.id);return}
  catch(err){if(m)m.textContent=err&&err.code?sampleErr(err):err&&err.message==='no'?'Claude couldn’t use that event. Try a different one.':'That came back in the wrong shape. Try again.'}
  TAV.busy=false}
/* hook into the Sociology screen */
(function(){var _sv=sjView;sjView=function(id){if(id==='soc'&&SJV.ta){if(SJV.qz||SJV.mm||SJV.rv||SJV.p)SJV.ta=null;else return taView()}_sv(id)};
  var _sh=sjHome;sjHome=function(id){_sh(id);if(id!=='soc')return;var L=taLean(),a=app.querySelector('.sub');
    if(a)a.insertAdjacentHTML('afterend','<div class="card tacard"><p class="tattl">Thinkers argue</p><p class="sub" style="margin:0 0 4px">Marx, Durkheim, Weber and Srinivas explain the IPL auction, Spotify Wrapped, a big wedding and more. Decide who is right, then argue back.'+(L.t?' You have judged '+L.t+' of '+taAll().length+'.':'')+'</p><button class="btn" style="text-align:center;background:var(--c5);color:var(--card)" onclick="taOpen()">'+(L.t?'Continue the arguments':'Start the first argument')+'</button></div>')};
  var _vt=viewTop;viewTop=function(v){_vt(v);if(v==='sj_soc')SJV.ta=null}})();
